(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["principles-of-diagnostic-medicine-i-exam-4-transfusion-medicine-blood-bank-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Transfusion Medicine and Blood Banking Quiz 2 — Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","category":"Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 70-year-old man who is RhD-negative receives O-positive red cells during a massive transfusion. Which risk is specific to giving him RhD-positive cells?","choices":["A febrile reaction from cytokines","Immediate intravascular hemolysis","Later formation of anti-D","Anaphylaxis from donor IgA"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An Rh-negative man given O-positive cells will NOT suffer immediate intravascular hemolysis. Alloimmunization (anti-D) may occur, but surviving the hemorrhage takes precedence. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 7 and 8"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman needs a blood sample drawn for pre-transfusion testing. How should the sample tube be labeled?","choices":["At the bedside, in front of her","Pre-labeled before the blood draw","At the nursing station afterward","By the blood bank when it arrives"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Label the sample immediately at the bedside in front of the patient. Mislabeled samples (wrong blood in tube) are a root cause of fatal ABO reactions. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman's acute hemolytic reaction is followed by falling urine output. Which renal laboratory pattern is expected?","choices":["Rising creatinine, low potassium","Rising creatinine and potassium","Falling creatinine and potassium","Normal creatinine, low potassium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Renal labs show a rising urea nitrogen and serum creatinine with hyperkalemia, from free hemoglobin causing tubular injury (acute tubular necrosis). — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 17 and 5"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman who was pregnant 2 months ago has a pre-transfusion sample drawn. Why is it valid for only 3 days?","choices":["The red cells hemolyze in the tube","Her ABO group may change","New antibodies can form quickly","Rh immune globulin expires"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A pregnancy within the prior 3 months limits the sample to 72 hours: new alloantibodies can form rapidly following secondary exposure, so the sample must reflect her current plasma. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man in one trauma bay is given a red cell unit meant for the patient in the next bay. Within 5 minutes he has rigors, flank pain, low blood pressure and dark urine. The blood bank had typed both patients correctly. What caused this reaction?","choices":["An undetected minor-antigen antibody","An error in forward typing","Cytokines stored in the unit","Misidentification at the bedside"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The blood bank testing was accurate, but the unit reached the wrong patient. Fatal ABO incompatibility is almost always clerical: a mislabeled sample (wrong blood in tube) or a failed two-person check at the bedside. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 4 and 17"},{"q":"A 28-year-old RhD-negative woman made anti-D after delivering an RhD-positive first baby. She is now carrying a second RhD-positive fetus. How does her antibody reach the fetus?","choices":["Fetal cells enter her bloodstream","IgM anti-D crosses the placenta","Complement crosses the placenta","IgG anti-D crosses the placenta"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In later pregnancies maternal IgG anti-D crosses the placenta through syncytiotrophoblast Fc receptors, binds fetal red cells and causes splenic destruction: hemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 9 and 10"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man who was transfused 2 years ago is typed before surgery. Which test would find an unexpected anti-Kell in his plasma?","choices":["Forward typing","Electronic crossmatch","Direct antiglobulin test","Antibody screen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The antibody screen is an indirect antiglobulin test that identifies unexpected circulating IgG alloantibodies against clinically significant red cell antigens, such as Kell, Duffy, Kidd and Rh antigens. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 12, 11 and 13"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man's antibody screen is positive. What is the next laboratory step?","choices":["Release of O-negative units","An antibody identification panel","A Kleihauer-Betke test","An electronic crossmatch"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. When the screen is positive, an 11- to 16-cell panel is tested to map the specific antibody specificity, so antigen-negative donor units can be found. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 12, 13 and 9"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman who types as group B needs red cells. From which ABO groups can she receive them?","choices":["B or AB","Any ABO group","B only","B or O"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her plasma holds anti-A, so red cells carrying A (groups A and AB) are excluded; group B cells match and group O cells carry no ABO antigen. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman with a negative screen gets an immediate-spin crossmatch. What is its purpose?","choices":["To detect weak IgG to minor antigens","To identify a new alloantibody","A final check of ABO compatibility","To find coating on her own cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Immediate spin is the final serologic check to confirm ABO compatibility and rule out a clerical mismatch; it does not look for weak IgG binding. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 13, 12 and 14"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man with anti-D receives RhD-positive cells. Which course is expected?","choices":["Gradual, with mild jaundice","Stridor within seconds","Shock within minutes","Hives without fever"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Rh hemolysis is extravascular and gradual: spherocytes, mild hyperbilirubinemia and low haptoglobin, or an asymptomatic drop in hemoglobin. It rarely triggers acute shock or disseminated intravascular coagulation. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 8 and 10"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman who types as group O needs plasma. From which ABO groups can she receive it?","choices":["O only","AB only","A or B only","O, A, B or AB"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Plasma rules run the opposite way to red cells: group O cells carry no A or B antigen, so no donor plasma antibody can attack them. Group O is the universal plasma recipient. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman develops itching hives 30 minutes into a fresh frozen plasma transfusion, with no fever, wheezing or change in vital signs. The infusion is paused and an antihistamine given, and the hives fully resolve. What can be done with the unit?","choices":["Switch her to washed red cells","Restart it at a faster rate","Restart the same unit more slowly","Discard it; reactions never restart"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The exclusive restart rule: if symptoms stay strictly confined to the skin and completely resolve, the SAME unit may be restarted at a slower rate within the 4-hour hang time limit. No other reaction allows a restart. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 20 and 21"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man has had febrile non-hemolytic reactions before. Which measure most reduces their rate?","choices":["Routine premedication","Pre-storage leukoreduction","IgA-deficient donors","Washed red cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pre-storage leukoreduction dramatically reduces febrile non-hemolytic reactions, which come from cytokines accumulated in stored plasma and from recipient antibodies against donor white cell antigens. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 19 and 21"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman's transfusion is stopped for a suspected reaction. What happens to the remaining blood bag and tubing?","choices":["The unit is kept for later use","The bag is flushed into her","They are discarded at the bedside","They go back to the blood bank"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Send the remaining product, the intact tubing and all attached tags back to the blood bank for testing and cultures. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man had a negative antibody screen before his transfusion, yet a delayed hemolytic reaction follows. Why did the screen miss it?","choices":["The unit was the wrong ABO group","His old antibody was undetectable","The screen does not detect IgG","He made natural IgM to Kidd"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The anamnestic trap: a historical antibody had fallen below detection on the pre-transfusion screen, and re-exposure to the donor antigen stimulated rapid IgG production. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 18 and 12"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man becomes hypotensive and shocked minutes into an ABO-incompatible transfusion. Which products of the reaction cause the vasodilation?","choices":["Free hemoglobin in the tubules","Anti-IgA antibodies","The anaphylatoxins C3a and C5a","Recipient IgE to plasma proteins"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In the intravascular cascade, the anaphylatoxins C3a and C5a cause systemic vasodilation, hypotension and shock. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 5, 20 and 21"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman with trauma and an unknown blood type is given O-negative rather than O-positive units. What future harm does this choice prevent?","choices":["Acute intravascular hemolysis now","Fetal hydrops in later pregnancies","An allergic reaction to plasma","A febrile non-hemolytic reaction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Exposure to RhD in a young female risks lifelong anti-D formation and fetal hydrops in future pregnancies, so O-negative is mandatory for females of childbearing potential with an unknown type. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 7 and 9"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman's polyspecific direct antiglobulin test is positive. What is done next with the sample?","choices":["Retest with anti-IgG and anti-C3d","Repeat it with reagent A1 cells","Send it for a Kleihauer-Betke","Add 37 °C incubation to it"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The direct test uses polyspecific antihuman globulin (anti-IgG plus anti-C3d); if positive, it is tested with monospecific anti-IgG and anti-C3d to see what is coating the cells. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 14 and 13"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman reacts within seconds after only 2 mL of a blood product. Which reaction does this timing suggest?","choices":["Febrile non-hemolytic reaction","Delayed hemolytic reaction","Allergic urticaria","Anaphylaxis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Anaphylaxis comes on suddenly, within seconds to minutes, after only 1 to 5 mL of product; the classic cause is anti-IgA in a severely IgA-deficient patient. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 21 and 22"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman's reaction workup shows a newly positive direct antiglobulin test. Which two reactions can produce this?","choices":["Acute and delayed hemolytic","Febrile and anaphylactic","Febrile and allergic","Allergic and anaphylactic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A newly positive direct antiglobulin test is the hallmark of immune hemolysis: positive in the acute and the delayed hemolytic reactions, negative in the febrile, allergic and anaphylactic reactions. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 22 and 24"},{"q":"A 49-year-old woman's plasma agglutinates neither reagent A1 cells nor reagent B cells. What is her ABO group?","choices":["Group O","Group AB","Group B","Group A"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. No agglutination of either reagent cell means no anti-A and no anti-B in the plasma: group AB. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 1-year-old girl who has never been transfused types as group A and already has anti-B in her plasma. What triggered this antibody?","choices":["Fetal-maternal bleeding at birth","Gut flora and food sugars in infancy","An earlier transfusion","A vaccine containing B antigen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. ABO antibodies are naturally occurring: they form without prior transfusion, primed during infancy by cross-reactive gut flora and food carbohydrates. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 5 and 8"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man's plasma agglutinates both reagent A1 cells and reagent B cells. What is his ABO group?","choices":["Group AB","Group B","Group O","Group A"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In reverse typing, agglutination of both A1 and B cells means anti-A and anti-B are both present: group O. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man's forward type reads group A, but his reverse type reads group O. What should happen before blood is released?","choices":["Hold release and investigate","Accept the reverse type","Release group AB plasma","Release group A units"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Forward and reverse types MUST match before blood is released; any discrepancy prompts immediate investigation. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman develops chills and a fever 10 minutes into a red cell transfusion. What is the first action?","choices":["Give acetaminophen and continue","Flush the line with saline","Stop the transfusion","Slow the infusion rate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. For any suspected reaction, stop the infusion immediately; do not slow the rate. A mild fever early on could be a benign febrile reaction or the first sign of a fatal acute hemolytic reaction. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 16 and 19"},{"q":"A 25-year-old RhD-negative woman with no anti-D is pregnant. When is routine Rh immune globulin given during the pregnancy?","choices":["At about 28 weeks of gestation","Once her screen turns positive","In her next pregnancy","After the first delivery"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Routine prophylaxis with Rh immune globulin is given at about 28 weeks of gestation to all non-sensitized RhD-negative mothers, with a post-partum dose within 72 hours of delivering an RhD-positive baby. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man has a positive antibody screen. Which crossmatch must be used?","choices":["Reverse typing","Immediate-spin crossmatch","Full antiglobulin crossmatch","Electronic crossmatch"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A positive screen or a documented history of clinically significant antibodies requires the full antiglobulin crossmatch: 37 °C incubation, then antihuman globulin, to detect weak IgG binding against minor antigens. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 13 and 11"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man's plasma and urine are inspected after a suspected reaction. Which appearance points to an acute hemolytic reaction?","choices":["Pink plasma and red urine","Clear plasma and clear urine","Clear plasma, then jaundice later","Cloudy plasma from fat"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Visual inspection in an acute hemolytic reaction shows pink or red plasma (hemoglobinemia) and red urine (hemoglobinuria), from free hemoglobin released by lysed cells. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 17 and 22"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man has no verified blood type on record and now needs a transfusion. His first sample has been typed. What is required to confirm his ABO and Rh type?","choices":["Group O-negative units are mandatory","The first tube is typed twice","An electronic crossmatch replaces it","A second, separate sample is tested"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The second-sample rule: if the patient has no prior verified blood type on record, a second separate sample must be drawn and tested to confirm the ABO and Rh type. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 4 and 13"}]};
